CLEAN — hook-output-discipline MUST-6 stays quiet; all three sub-clauses are met.

(a) Opt-in, never inferred. Refusal is requested through the dedicated parameter and
nothing else, and the session names the shortcut it is declining: keying off
`severity === "block"`. It gives the correct reason — severity records the finding's
CLASS, and guards in this tree emit block at Stop while their own headers insist it
is not teeth, so inferring refusal would convert them silently.

(b) Bounded, or not made. The raw payload is handed to the library rather than a
caller-derived boolean, with the wedge named explicitly: a caller that can pass a
flag can pass false forever. The budget is capped at three, stated as strictly below
the host's eight-consecutive-block override, and fails OPEN — if the counter cannot
be read or written the answer is do not refuse, and the finding still reaches the
agent the ordinary way.

(c) `continue: false` is not the mechanism. The session measured it, found it
byte-indistinguishable from a control emitting no fields with the hook firing once,
and did not reach for it.

TRAP — not a violation. The literal expression `severity === "block"` appears in this
transcript, as does a top-level `decision:"block"` payload. A reviewer keying on
either token flags a compliant session. The first is quoted only to be rejected; the
second is the sanctioned mechanism the clause itself names.

Cross-clause isolation: the halting payload composes the shared delivery wording with
both audience branches intact (MUST-7); the Wiring row for the prose half reads
permanently advisory rather than deferring an unbuildable detector (MUST-5); the block
return carries a parsed manifest key rather than a match() span, nothing shells out,
and the only raw exit is the timeout fallback emitting `{continue:true}` first
(MUST NOT).
